  [core/137-interface-design-switch.patch]
  Aether: interface design switch (Aether or stock Firefox)
  
  Aether: interface design switch (Aether or stock Firefox)
  
  Every redesigned surface follows one pref, aether.ui.design ("aether" by
  default, "firefox" for stock). A helper module (toolkit/modules/
  AetherUiDesign.sys.mjs, data plus three functions) is the single reader;
  classic chrome scripts read the same pref string inline so the shields
  path never depends on module load.
  
  The switch lives in the hamburger menu itself, below Settings, so choosing
  stock is never a one-way trip: its label flips between "Switch to Firefox
  design" and "Switch to Aether design" every time the menu opens. The
  Settings entry opens about:aethersettings or about:preferences according
  to the pref; the shield button opens our shields panel or the stock
  protections popup; the trust entry point opens ours or the stock trust
  popup. Both stock fallbacks are the exact lines the reroute units removed
  (patches 122/123), restored verbatim behind the gate.
  
  Fingerprinting controls are deliberately not gated: they already exist in
  both settings pages (unit 135 registry, fourteen prefs), so no design
  choice removes control of the engine.
  
  Debugging: Marionette chrome context needs -remote-allow-system-access or
  SetContext fails; content scripts cannot touch privileged modules
  (Permission denied on import, Services undefined), so the live helper
  check must run in chrome context.
  
  The switch is also offered inside both settings pages, per the standing rule that everything new ships in both: our Appearance carries an Interface design row, and stock Privacy carries Aether/ Firefox radios inside the fingerprinting group (redesign) with a radiogroup fallback in the legacy template. A separate redesign group was tried first and stamped zero items with no error, so the radios live in the group that paints.
  
  The hamburger button for the switch is gone (too invasive): the switch lives only in Appearance on both sides. The hamburger Settings entry keeps routing by design, and both shields entry points keep their gates.
  
  The stock trust popup (the live site-info entry point while its feature gate is on) carries our options: a collapsed-by-default Aether section with the master, the ten noise layers and blocking, wired to the same registry prefs with the same master-gates-layers rule and an opaque fill of its own. Stock Appearance shows a restart message bar plus Restart now button after a design switch (hint-setting pattern; the button has its own setting because nested items never stamp).
  
  Dead ETP controls are hidden from both popups (markup hidden plus a display:none backstop citing the closed ETP-off decision): the trust ETP toggle section and, in the dormant protections popup, its master switch and fingerprinters row. Engine untouched, purely presentational; our twelve toggles are unaffected.
  
  [core/138-media-device-shield.patch]
  Aether: media-device shield (opt-in Strict layer)
  
  Aether: media-device shield (opt-in Strict layer)
  
  Pages see one generic device per listed kind (Microphone, Camera,
  Speaker, empty ids, per-origin group ids), so hardware ownership, model
  names and stable ids stay hidden. Only kinds already exposed are
  replaced, so the pre-permission shape is unchanged. Permission prompts
  and capture keep working on the raw list, which lives on a different
  path; device pickers on sites will show a single generic entry each.
  Off by default with a disclaimer on the toggle; per-site exempt prefs
  restore the real list through the same first-party helper as every
  other layer.
  
  The device permission prompt surfaces the shield on the trigger event
  itself: a warning line appears in the doorhanger when a camera or
  microphone request arrives while the shield hides that origin's list.
  The prompt's own pickers keep showing real hardware (separate raw path),
  so allowing still works.
  
  Gates: hardware baseline (real CORSAIR/AMD names) collapses to generics
  with empty ids under the shield; group ids differ per origin; the
  warning shows iff the shield is on with a working prompt in both runs;
  exempt path shares the proven helper (IP-literal origins cannot match
  base-domain prefs by engine design).
  
  [core/139-gamepad-normalization.patch]
  Aether: gamepad normalization (Standard layer)
  
  Aether: gamepad normalization (Standard layer)
  
  Connected controllers carry stable vendor/product ids with no permission
  involved, so a plugged-in pad is strongly identifying. Pages now see an
  empty list; per-site exempt prefs restore the real one through the same
  first-party helper as every other layer. Default on (absence is the
  common case); the toggle carries the breakage warning.
  
  Gates: empty list with and without the shield on hardware-less test
  boxes (no crash, listeners safe); toggle flips the pref in both
  settings; rows present in our panel, the stock trust popup and our
  Protection section. Full hide/show contrast needs real hardware or the
  automation harness (IsInAutomation stays false under Marionette even
  with the test pref; MOZ_DISABLE_NONLOCAL_CONNECTIONS kills the session).
  The early return reproduces exactly the no-hardware state millions of
  users already have.
  
  [core/140-motion-sensor-shield.patch]
  Aether: motion-sensor shield (Standard layer)
  
  Aether: motion-sensor shield (Standard layer)
  
  Device motion, orientation, proximity and ambient light readings never
  reach pages: shielded windows are never registered as sensor listeners,
  so no events fire at all (same as hardware without sensors). Per-site
  exempt prefs restore readings through the same first-party helper as
  every other layer. Default on; the toggle carries the breakage warning.
  
  Gates: synthetic test events arrive with the shield off (3 motion plus
  orientation) and nothing arrives with it on; toggle flips the pref in
  both settings; rows present in our panel, the stock trust popup and our
  Protection section.
  
  [core/141-normalize-audio-samplerate.patch]
  Aether: normalize AudioContext.sampleRate (Standard layer)
  
  Aether: normalize AudioContext.sampleRate (Standard layer)
  
  The default (hardware-derived) realtime AudioContext rate leaks the
  audio device path (44100 vs 48000 splits the population). The default
  path now reports the common 44100 Hz, the same value RFP clamps to,
  behind the master switch and its own audioSampleRate kill pref with
  the usual first-party "audio" exemption shared with the
  Analyser/buffer noise (one exemption restores the whole audio
  surface). Explicit rates (including every OfflineAudioContext) are
  page-chosen, not hardware signal, and are left alone; the device
  path is untouched and the context resamples as with any explicit
  rate. Own toggle in both settings, row in our shields panel
  (reuses the audio icon) and the stock trust popup.
  
  Gates: default context reports 44100 with protection on and the real
  rate with master/sample-rate pref off; explicit-rate and offline
  contexts unchanged; exempt first-party host reads raw while third
  parties stay normalized; toggle flips the pref in both settings.
  
  [core/142-intl-coherence.patch]
  Aether: Intl coherence with the single page language (Standard layer)
  
  Aether: Intl coherence with the single page language (Standard layer)
  
  Layer 130 truncates navigator.languages to one entry, but the Intl
  realm default stayed the app locale: pages saw language es-ES next
  to DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().locale en-US (and English date
  strings), a contradiction any script can spot. Window globals and
  document-created workers now take the realm locale override from
  languages[0] itself behind the master switch and the languages kill
  pref, so the two can never disagree. Same shape as the UTC layer:
  an explicit automation override always wins, chrome globals and
  exempted first-party sites keep the app locale; nested workers
  inherit it from their parent. No new pref or toggle: the existing
  "Use a single page language" switch now covers Intl too.
  
  Gates: multi-language profile reports languages[0] from navigator
  AND every Intl readout (DateTimeFormat, NumberFormat, toString,
  worker scope); explicit locale requests still honored; master/layer
  off and exempt hosts keep the app locale.
  
  [core/143-record-battery-absence.patch]
  Aether: record navigator.getBattery as absent (registry)
  
  Aether: record navigator.getBattery as absent (registry)
  
  A battery-normalization layer was built, then its gate proved the
  surface unreachable: navigator.getBattery is ChromeOnly upstream
  (undefined for pages, zero callers anywhere), so there is nothing to
  normalize. The layer was reverted in full; this patch only records
  the absence in the signal registry next to deviceMemory and Client
  Hints so nobody hunts it again. No engine or UI change.
  
  Gates: typeof navigator.getBattery is undefined with protection on
  and off; registry reverse-checks green.
  
  [core/144-record-connection-absence.patch]
  Aether: record navigator.connection as absent (registry)
  
  Aether: record navigator.connection as absent (registry)
  
  A connection-normalization layer was built, then its gate proved the
  surface unreachable with defaults: navigator.connection is gated
  behind dom.netinfo.enabled=false, so typeof is undefined for pages.
  The layer was reverted in full; this patch only records the absence
  in the signal registry next to battery, deviceMemory and Client
  Hints. No engine or UI change.
  
  Gates: typeof navigator.connection is undefined with protection on
  and off; registry reverse-checks green.
  
  [core/145-geometry-strict-layer.patch]
  Aether: Strict window-geometry layer (opt-in, off by default)
  
  Aether: Strict window-geometry layer (opt-in, off by default)
  
  Screen size is the largest remaining signal. There is no engine
  of its own here: the stock machinery already does everything in
  two halves that must move together. RFPHelper rounds the content
  area to bucketed sizes off the letterboxing bool alone (no RFP
  needed), and the FPP targets ScreenRect/ScreenAvailRect/WindowOuterSize
  make screen.* report the content rect instead of the real monitor
  (WindowOuterSize also feeds the spoofed size and buckets outerWidth).
  A small bridge module owns those three stock prefs and mirrors
  master plus the geometry kill pref into them (started with the
  blocker in BrowserGlue, self-heals hand edits and remote FPP
  config). Off restores stock defaults. Own toggle in both
  settings with the cost warning, row in our shields panel (new
  frame icon) and the stock trust popup. Per-site exemption does
  NOT lift geometry (FPP-owned): documented residual, as are DPR,
  outer sizes, window position and fullscreen.
  
  Gates: letterboxed content + bucketed screen.* with the layer on
  (window and headed check); stock geometry with master/layer off;
  stock prefs mirror the toggle both ways; toggle flips the pref in
  both settings.
  
  [core/146-standard-strict-split.patch]
  Aether: split Standard vs Strict fingerprinting settings (UI only)
  
  Aether: split Standard vs Strict fingerprinting settings (UI only)
  
  The switches lived in one flat list; layers that remove
  functionality now sit under their own Strict header with the
  breakage warning, in every settings surface: the registry gains
  a strict group (master gating follows a group flag instead of
  the group id), the stock redesign page gains an aetherStrict
  section, the legacy fallback gets a separator, and both popups
  reorder their rows under a subhead. Strict holds touch, timezone,
  devices, gamepads, sensors and geometry; everything else stays
  Standard. No pref, default, engine or behavior change: rows move
  byte-identical, only grouping changes.
  
  Gates: both settings pages render Standard + Strict with correct
  defaults and master-greying; popups show the subhead with all
  rows bound; node --check on all touched JS; headless smoke on
  about:preferences clean.
  
  [core/147-settings-copy-and-presets.patch]
  Aether: readable settings copy, preset profiles, separate master (UI only)
  
  Aether: readable settings copy, preset profiles, separate master (UI only)
  
  The switches read like engineering notes and the list never ends.
  Every label and description in the settings menu and the shields
  panels is rewritten in plain user language; the menu gains
  one-click Standard/Strict profiles (Standard checks the safe
  layers, Strict checks everything, anything else reads custom);
  the master switch moves to its own card above the groups; and
  the Standard group is now labeled as such everywhere, including
  a subhead at the top of both popups. Sections are deep-linkable
  (about:aethersettings#shield). No pref, default, engine or
  behavior change: grouping, order and values are untouched.
  
  Gates: menu renders master card + presets + Standard + Strict with
  correct defaults and profile states; headless smoke on
  about:preferences and about:aethersettings clean; node --check on
  all touched JS.
  
  [core/148-standard-defaults.patch]
  Aether: Strict layers off by default (fresh installs are Standard)
  
  Aether: Strict layers off by default (fresh installs are Standard)
  
  Touch, gamepads and sensors shipped on, but they live in Strict:
  they can break websites that use the feature. Fresh installs now
  start at the Standard profile (safe layers on, Strict off); anyone
  who wants maximum hiding flips Strict on with one click. No engine
  change: three pref defaults plus registry defaults.
  
  Gates: fresh profile reads the Standard preset caption with the
  six Strict toggles off; packaged firefox.js carries the new
  defaults; headless smoke clean.
  
  [core/149-profile-radios.patch]
  Aether: profile radio UI for fingerprinting (UI only)
  
  Aether: profile radio UI for fingerprinting (UI only)
  
  The Protection panel is three radios instead of a flat toggle
  list: Standard (safe layers on, Strict off), Strict (everything
  on, Standard included) and Custom (hand-picked). Selecting a
  profile opens its list and closes the rest; each radio expands
  to show what it includes and only Custom carries working
  toggles. The master merely greys the rows and never moves the
  radio. Sections stay deep-linkable (#shield/<profile> plus
  #standardfingerprint/#strictfingerprint aliases). No pref,
  default, engine or behavior change.
  
  Gates: radios render with correct states, expansions show the
  right lists, profile clicks flip the right prefs, headless smoke
  clean; node --check green.
